.TH PDE 2
.SH NAME
pde: new, clear, get, set, splat, splatold, diffuse, wave, gray \- finite-difference field solvers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "pde.m";
pde := load Pde Pde->PATH;
Field: import pde;

f := pde->new(nx, ny, dx, dy, Pde->CLAMP);
pde->diffuse(f, diffusivity, dt);
pde->wave(f, speed, damping, dt);
pde->gray(a, b, da, db, feed, kill, dt);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pde
provides reusable explicit finite-difference solvers on rectangular scalar
fields.  A Field owns its current values, a work buffer, and the previous
time level required by the wave solver.  Values are stored row-major in
.BR u .
.PP
.B New
creates an nx by ny field with physical cell sizes dx and dy.  Boundary mode
.B CLAMP
imposes a zero normal derivative,
.B PERIODIC
wraps both axes, and
.B ZERO
uses zero outside the domain.
.B Clear,
.B Get,
.B Set,
.B Splat,
and
.B Splatold
initialize or interact with a field.
.PP
.B Diffuse
solves the two-dimensional heat equation.
.B Wave
solves a damped second-order wave equation; its Field's old array is the
previous time level.
.B Gray
advances two equally sized fields with the Gray-Scott reaction-diffusion
model.  All three routines automatically subdivide dt to satisfy a
conservative explicit stability limit.
.SS "The problem-specification language"
.EX
(p, err) := pde->newproblem(
	"mesh 64x64 domain 1x1 bc clamp\en" +
	"equation diffuse diffusivity 0.05\en" +
	"solver gmres tolerance 1e-8 restart 30\en" +
	"time step 0.01");
if(err != nil)
	...
err = pde->step(p, dt);
(nsteps, err) = pde->run(p, 10.0);
.EE
.B Newproblem
parses a spec into a ready
.B Problem
in one call, the same relationship a printf format string has to what
it produces.  A spec is one or more newline-separated lines:
.TP
.BI "mesh " NXxNY " [domain " WxH "] [bc clamp|periodic|zero]"
Delegates to
.IR mesh (2);
see there for the grammar.  Required.
.TP
.BI "equation diffuse diffusivity " D
.TP
.BI "equation wave speed " S " damping " Dm
.TP
.BI "equation gray da " Da " db " Db " feed " F " kill " K
Selects the equation and its parameters.  Required.
.B Gray
is the one equation needing two coupled fields: newproblem() allocates
a second field the same size as the first, reachable as
.IR p . fieldb
(the first is
.IR p . field ,
as for every other equation \(em existing single-field callers keep
working unchanged).  Its own reaction term is nonlinear, so unlike
.B diffuse
and
.B wave
it has no implicit solver here; only
.B "solver explicit"
is valid for it.
.TP
.BI "equation hoc " NAME " [" NAMEB ]
The update rule itself comes from the caller, as
.IR hoc (2)
expression strings, rather than one of the three fixed equations above
\(em a caller WRITES the PDE (Turing patterns, FitzHugh-Nagumo,
anything of this reaction-diffusion shape) instead of supplying
numbers into an equation this module already hard-coded.  There are no
coefficient fields for it: every constant an expression needs is a
named
.B param
line, not an
.I adt
field.
.RS
.TP
.BI "substeps " N
Required.  Unlike
.BR diffuse / wave / gray ,
there is no general stability heuristic to derive a substep count from
for an arbitrary user rhs, so it is explicit.
.TP
.BI "param " NAME " " VALUE
Repeatable.  Binds a hoc variable any
.B rhs
line may reference.
.TP
.BI "rhs " NAME " " hoc-expression
One per field name given on the
.B "equation hoc"
line, after it in the spec.  The rest of the line is taken verbatim as
hoc source \(em unlike every other line here, it is not word-split, so
an expression may contain spaces freely.  Inside it,
.I NAME
is that field's own current value,
.BI lap_ NAME
is its five-point Laplacian (computed the same fast way
.BR diffuse / wave / gray
already do \(em only the per-cell update itself is hoc-driven),
.B h
is the current substep's own dt, and any
.B param
is that name's value.
.RE
.IP
Evaluated with
.IR hoc (2)'s
.BR evalarray() ,
the same parsed tree
.B eval()
would walk scalar-at-a-time, walked once per substep over the whole
grid instead.
.I Hoc
has no clamp/min/max builtin, so a
.B rhs
wanting bounded values (as
.BR gray 's
own
.RI [0,1]
species fractions are) must express that itself, or accept unbounded
output \(em it is not silently clamped to
.RI [0,1]
the way
.B gray
itself is, since that clamp is specific to what
.BR gray 's
own fields mean, not a general property of this equation kind.  Like
.BR gray ,
only
.B "solver explicit"
is valid \(em the update rule is arbitrary, not necessarily linear.
Requires an
.B "equation hoc"
line before any of its own
.BR substeps / param / rhs
lines, since they refer to the field name(s) it declares.
.TP
.B "solver explicit"
The default: the same stability-limited
.B diffuse
/
.B wave
/
.B gray
routines described above, internally subdividing dt as needed.
.TP
.BI "solver gmres|cg [tolerance " T "] [restart " R "] [maxiter " N ]
Solves
.B diffuse
or
.B wave
implicitly via
.IR krylov (2)
instead, matrix-free, unconditionally stable for any dt \(em unlike
.BR explicit ,
it never needs to subdivide the requested step.  Rejected at
.B newproblem
time for
.B gray
(see above).  For
.B diffuse
this is ordinary backward Euler; for
.B wave
it is the same central-difference-in-time update
.B explicit
uses, with the Laplacian moved onto the new time level (a single
linear solve for the new position, not a larger indirect system),
which keeps
.BR Field 's
.B old
array meaning exactly what
.B splatold
and explicit
.B wave
already document \(em the previous time level, not a second,
solver-dependent convention.  Both operators are symmetric positive
definite for
.B clamp
or
.B periodic
boundaries (each is the identity minus a positive multiple of the same
negative-semidefinite discrete Laplacian), so
.B cg
is valid (and faster) there;
.B gmres
is the safe general default (zero boundaries, or a future
non-symmetric equation).  Krylov internals never cross this interface
\(em nothing outside
.I pde.b
itself ever sees a
.IR krylov (2)
type.
.TP
.BI "time step " DT
Sets the default step
.B run
uses; optional, but required before calling
.BR run .
.PP
.B Step
advances
.I p
by an explicit
.I dt
(an interactive caller, e.g. a redraw loop, picks its own
.I dt
each call).
.B Run
is the batch/unattended driver: it repeats
.B step
using
.IR p 's
own
.B "time step"
until at least
.I until
total time has elapsed, returning the step count \(em what a
verification or UQ study, run without a redraw loop, calls instead of
.BR step .
Both return an error string (e.g. a solver that failed to converge)
rather than silently leaving
.I p.field
holding a garbage state.
